why 2 1/4 LEAPS The alto climbs E–F♯–G♯–A♯ by step to the leading tone. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 2 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence in B minor where every resolution falls: soprano C♯–B, the alto's A♯ drops to F♯, the seventh E to D and the bass to B.
why 3 1/8 DOUBLING A passing chord over a held bass; its doubling follows the rising upper voices.
why 3 1/8 SIX_FOUR Over the bass's held B the upper voices step up through a diminished chord (A♯–C♯–E) into B minor again: a passing chord over a pedal.
why 3 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices rise into B minor while the bass leaps an octave on B. The harmony returns to the tonic, so the shared direction loses nothing.
why 3 1/2 OVERLAP The alto drops a fifth to E, below where the tenor just was, while the tenor falls to C♯. They move down together, so the order stays clear.
why 3 5/8 LEAPS After its diminished leap down to C♯ the tenor keeps falling B–A instead of turning back, descending through the chords toward the cadence.
why 4 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (E–F♯) and alto (G–F♯) meet on F♯ from opposite sides by step.
why 5 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps up an augmented fourth, E to A♯, to put the leading tone in the bass for V⁶. A sharp, expressive leap; A♯ resolves to B at once.
why 6 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The same cadence as in bar 2: every resolution falls into B minor.
why 8 1/8 DOUBLING In this short cadential ⁶₄ soprano and tenor share B, each moving on to C♯ and A♯ in V⁷.
why 8 1/8 SIX_FOUR The cadential ⁶₄ is squeezed into one eighth on a weak part of the beat, with the bass's neighbor G after it: a quick, decorated approach to the final V⁷.
why 8 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on B major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the seventh E to D♯, the tenor's A♯ to F♯ and the bass to B.
